158:- The main chip: one supply for every I/O. The CAAA package has two VDD balls (A4, E10), no other supply ball for I/O (`parts/nrf54lm20a-csp61-pins.csv`: the supply-type names are VDD, VSS, VBUS and the decoupling balls DECD, DECA, DECRF, DECUSB). Nordic's reference layout says "VDD:nRF supplied by 1.8 V to 3.6 V" (`parts/nordic-ref-text/caaa_schematic.txt` line 55). So the chip's I/O for D9, A9, B10, D10 follows A4 and E10, which are on VDD_1V8 (1.8 V). The chip's I/O CAN run at 3.0 V only if A4 and E10 are moved to VDD_3V0, which moves every chip pin to 3.0 V: the microphone clock into 1.8 V microphones (VDD_MIC), the power chip's I2C (VDDIO), and the screen's IOVCC. That is a design change to the whole rail plan, not a wiring detail: not done here.
162:- So a level shifter: TI SN74AXC4T774, orderable code SN74AXC4T774RSVR (UQFN-16 RSV, 2.6 x 1.8 mm, 3000 per reel, Active, Production), read from TI's datasheet SCES898C fetched from `https://www.ti.com/lit/ds/symlink/sn74axc4t774.pdf` (HTTP 200, `evidence/a6/curl_axc4t774.log`; copy `evidence/a6/dl_ti/ti_sn74axc4t774.pdf`, text `evidence/a6/ti_sn74axc4t774.txt`, orderable table line 4136, pin table line 279). Each port 0.65 to 3.6 V; independent direction pins (function table line 3733: DIR high = A to B); VCC isolation: below 100 mV on either supply every port is Hi-Z (line 3684), so the part is safe while VDD_SD is switched off; 0.1 uF bypass on each supply (line 3813). Wiring: VCCA on VDD_1V8, VCCB on VDD_SD, DIR1 to DIR3 on VDD_1V8 (A to B: SCK, MOSI, CS), DIR4 on ground (B to A: MISO), OE on ground; A side = the chip nets, B side = the card nets with the W3 pull-ups. No supplier was contacted; the part was read, not priced.
